34,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"It seems we are spending about 2,300 yen monthly on supplies, often duplicating purchases. Also, the management office charges 4,200 yen per hour for cleaners, with one person handling the billing.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"The actual time for a full pass is over an hour and a half. Also, an outside firm requires us to tidy up beforehand and has a wide arrival window, which seems inefficient.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"My findings suggest a significant imbalance. A few of us do the bulk of the cooking, while others barely use the kitchen. Furthermore, the calendar shows frequent, lengthy absences among several housemates.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,May,,"It's no wonder the quality is all over the place. We have completely different standards for what ""done"" actually means, and the bathroom grout gets dirty again in less than two weeks regardless of effort.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Midori,,"It's worrying that our past arguments started with reminders about chores. Also, the previous tenants tried permanent assignments, but it ended with them barely speaking. We should be very careful about that.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"The duplication of supplies suggests a lack of coordination. If we choose a paid service, we must ensure the billing process is transparent so one person isn't left burdened with the cost.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"The time commitment is significant, and the outside firm's terms are restrictive. If we outsource, we're still doing the tidying, which doesn't fully remove the burden from our schedules. We need a solution that actually saves time.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"If some of us are rarely here or barely use the facilities, a rigid rotation seems unfair. We should consider whether the workload should align more closely with actual usage and presence.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,May,,"Exactly! And if we just rotate, we'll keep fighting because some people think a quick wipe is enough while others do a deep clean. We need a standard that everyone actually agrees on.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@May, I agree. If we don't have a shared understanding of the work, we'll just end up frustrated. We need a system that prioritizes our relationships and prevents those reminders that lead to arguments.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"If we want to avoid the friction Midori mentioned, we should probably avoid a system that relies on constant reminders. A clear, pre-set financial or structural arrangement would be more stable.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"If we go with a rota, the time burden remains high and unpredictable. However, the outside firm's requirements mean we don't actually outsource the effort, just the scrubbing. We need to weigh the cost against the actual hours saved.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"Given the data on absences and usage, a fixed rota would likely lead to resentment. Those who are rarely here would be paying in labor for a space they don't use, or conversely, skipping their turns.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,May,,"And if we do permanent assignments, we're just stuck with the same rooms. If I'm stuck with the bathroom, I'm fighting that grout every eleven days forever. That sounds like a nightmare.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@May, I completely understand. And based on what the previous tenants wrote, permanent assignments might actually damage our friendships over time. I think we should look for a way to share the burden without creating permanent roles.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"It seems we are all leaning away from permanent assignments. If we want to avoid resentment and financial confusion, we need a system that is either strictly professional or very clearly defined.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,"The time cost is too high for a casual arrangement, but the professional service isn't a magic fix. We need to decide if the financial cost is worth the time we'd actually save.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"If we want to avoid the resentment caused by unequal usage and absences, a professional service might be the most logical way to decouple the work from our personal habits and schedules.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,May,,"I agree with Benjamin. If we can't even agree on what ""clean"" means, we're just going to keep judging each other. Paying someone else to do it removes the guesswork and the fighting.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Midori,,"I agree. If a professional service removes the need for us to police each other and prevents the kind of friction that ruined the previous tenants' friendship, it seems like the kindest choice for our home.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
